\b\fs28 CHAPTER THREE\b0\fs24\par
\par
\b\fs28 SYSTEM ANALYSIS AND DESIGN\b0\fs24\par
\par
\b 3.0 Introduction\b0\par
\par
This chapter presents the system analysis and design of the proposed Anti-Forgery Proof Certification Registry for Skill Acquisition Using Smart Contracts and Artificial Intelligence. The purpose of the chapter is to translate the problem identified in Chapter One and the research gap identified in Chapter Two into a structured system design that can be implemented, tested, and evaluated. The chapter follows the system analysis and design principles discussed by Dennis, Wixom and Roth in Systems Analysis and Design with UML. Their text explains that information systems projects normally pass through planning, analysis, design and implementation phases, and that the analyst must move from business need to requirements, models, architecture, interface, data storage and implementation deliverables. This chapter applies those concepts to the SkillCert system.\par
\par
The design approach used in this study is object-oriented and prototype-driven. It models the system from the viewpoints of its major actors: learners, issuers, public verifiers, the AI assessment service, the backend application, the IPFS storage layer and the blockchain registry. The design also considers the security and trust assumptions of the system because the goal is not only to build a certificate portal, but to build a certification workflow in which a learner is assessed before certification and the issued certificate can be verified independently after issuance.\par
\par
In line with the analysis phase described by Dennis, Wixom and Roth, the chapter begins by analysing the existing certification process and its limitations. It then specifies the functional and non-functional requirements of the proposed system, models the system using UML-based descriptions, and presents the architecture, input design, output design, database design, process design and security design of the implemented system.\par
\par
\b 3.1 System Analysis\b0\par
\par
System analysis is the phase in which the existing situation is studied, problems are identified, requirements are discovered, and the desired behaviour of the new system is defined. Dennis, Wixom and Roth explain that requirements determination is one of the most important activities of the analysis phase because the analyst must understand what users need and convert those needs into clear functional requirements. In this project, the system analysis focuses on how certificates are currently issued, why such certificates are vulnerable to forgery, and how artificial intelligence and blockchain can be combined to improve trust.\par
\par
\b 3.1.1 Analysis of the Existing System\b0\par
\par
The existing system of certificate issuance in many skill acquisition and academic training environments is largely issuer-centred and document-centred. A learner completes a programme, the issuerevaluates the learner using its internal process, and a paper or digital certificate is issued. Verification is usually performed by visually inspecting the document, contacting the issuing issuer, checking a manually maintained database, or relying on signatures, seals and letterheads.\par
\par
This approach has several weaknesses. First, the certificate itself is often treated as the main proof of competence, even though the assessment evidence behind it may not be available to employers or third parties. Second, once a certificate is presented outside the issuing issuer, it may be difficult to confirm whether it is authentic, altered, revoked or issued by an authorized officer. Third, where verification depends on manual issueral communication, the process can be slow, inconsistent and difficult to audit. Finally, traditional systems may not provide a strong technical link between learner assessment and certificate issuance. A certificate may therefore appear valid even when the evidence of competence is weak, incomplete or unverifiable.\par
\par
The existing system can be summarized as follows:\par
\par
1. Learner attends a skill acquisition or academic programme.\par
2. issuerevaluates the learner using internal procedures.\par
3. issuerissues a physical or ordinary digital certificate.\par
4. Learner presents the certificate to an employer or verifier.\par
5. Verifier contacts the issueror inspects the certificate manually.\par
6. issuerconfirms or rejects the certificate if records are available.\par
\par
This workflow places high trust in the issuing issuerand in the physical or digital document. It does not provide independent cryptographic proof of authenticity, does not provide automated certificate revocation, and does not ensure that assessment evidence is linked to the certificate.\par
\par
\b 3.1.2 Limitations of the Existing System\b0\par
\par
The limitations of the existing system are:\par
\par
1. Certificate forgery and alteration: Paper certificates and ordinary digital documents can be duplicated, edited or falsely produced.\par
2. Slow verification: Employers and other verifiers may need to contact issuers manually, which delays recruitment and decision-making.\par
3. Weak audit trail: Manual issuance and verification processes do not always record who issued a certificate, when it was issued, what evidence supported it, or whether it has been revoked.\par
4. Lack of transparent revocation: A certificate may remain in circulation even after the issuerwithdraws it.\par
5. Separation of assessment and certificate issuance: Many systems do not technically enforce that the learner must pass a validated assessment before receiving a certificate.\par
6. Limited privacy control: Existing public certificate lists may expose more learner information than necessary.\par
7. Centralized trust: The verifier depends entirely on the issuer's internal database or staff response.\par
8. Poor scalability: Manual verification becomes inefficient as the number of learners, issuers and certificates increases.\par
\par
These limitations show the need for a system that supports secure learner registration, controlled course enrollment, AI-assisted assessment, automatic certificate issuance after successful assessment, blockchain-based verification, and privacy-preserving public registry statistics.\par
\par
\b 3.1.3 Objectives of the Proposed System\b0\par
\par
The proposed system is designed to achieve the following objectives:\par
\par
1. To provide a secure web-based platform for learners and issuers.\par
2. To enable issuers to create courses and assessment templates.\par
3. To restrict learner enrollment through issuer-generated course codes.\par
4. To generate thirty assessment questions from course material using AI-assisted methods.\par
5. To prevent learners from choosing or manipulating assessment difficulty.\par
6. To grade learner answers using a hybrid LLM and local rule-based method.\par
7. To issue certificates only after a learner passes the assessment.\par
8. To mint certificate NFTs through a smart contract registry.\par
9. To store certificate metadata and evidence using IPFS where configured.\par
10. To provide public verification using token ID and verification code.\par
11. To support certificate revocation while preserving an audit trail.\par
12. To protect sensitive learner data by exposing only limited public verification information.\par
\par
\b 3.2 System Requirements Specification\b0\par
\par
The system requirements specification states what the system must do and the quality attributes it must satisfy. In the analysis approach described by Dennis, Wixom and Roth, requirements are derived from user needs, business rules and technical constraints. For this project, requirements were derived from the anti-forgery problem, the needs of learners and issuers, the need for public verification, and the technical properties of AI, blockchain and IPFS.\par
\par
\b 3.2.1 Functional Requirements\b0\par
\par
The functional requirements are grouped by module.\par
\par
\b Authentication and User Management\b0\par
\par
1. The system shall allow learners to register with email, password and wallet address.\par
2. The system shall allow issuers to register with email, password, issuername and wallet address.\par
3. The system shall authenticate users through login.\par
4. The system shall issue access tokens and refresh tokens for authenticated sessions.\par
5. The system shall protect learner and issuerdashboards from unauthenticated access.\par
6. The system shall allow logout and session invalidation.\par
\par
\b Course and Enrollment Management\b0\par
\par
1. The issuershall create courses with title, description and status.\par
2. The issuershall generate one or more enrollment codes for a course.\par
3. The learner shall view available courses without seeing protected course materials.\par
4. The learner shall enroll in a course by entering a valid issuer-provided code.\par
5. The backend shall validate code status, expiry and quota before enrollment.\par
6. The backend shall record learner enrollment date and course relationship.\par
\par
\b Material and Assessment Template Management\b0\par
\par
1. The issuershall upload or enter course material.\par
2. The backend shall send material to the AI service for ingestion.\par
3. The AI service shall extract key concepts from the material.\par
4. The issuershall create multiple assessment templates for the same course.\par
5. Each assessment template shall be linked to a course, material and issuer.\par
6. Each assessment shall use exactly thirty questions.\par
\par
\b Assessment Generation\b0\par
\par
1. The learner shall select an assessment template from an enrolled course.\par
2. The backend shall confirm learner enrollment before creating an assessment.\par
3. The backend shall randomly assign or control difficulty without learner selection.\par
4. The AI service shall attempt to generate questions using OpenAI LLM.\par
5. The AI service shall validate that generated questions follow the required distribution: eight definition, eight conceptual, seven application and seven analysis questions.\par
6. The AI service shall fall back to local generation if LLM generation fails, times out or returns invalid JSON.\par
7. The learner-facing response shall not expose expected answers or rubrics.\par
\par
\b Answer Submission and Grading\b0\par
\par
1. The learner shall answer each assessment question.\par
2. The backend shall store answer submissions.\par
3. The backend shall send grading requests to the AI service.\par
4. The AI service shall grade answers locally and semantically where LLM grading is available.\par
5. The final hybrid score shall prioritize semantic grading while retaining local grading as a consistency signal.\par
6. The AI service shall return detailed results, feedback, score breakdown, outcome and assessment report hash.\par
7. The backend shall store assessment result and detailed grading output.\par
\par
\b Certificate Issuance and Verification\b0\par
\par
1. The backend shall issue a certificate only if the assessment outcome is PASS.\par
2. The backend shall generate certificate metadata and a verification code.\par
3. The backend shall create a certificate PDF.\par
4. The backend shall pin certificate evidence to IPFS where configured.\par
5. The backend shall call the smart contract registry to issue the certificate NFT.\par
6. The system shall store token ID, transaction hash, metadata CID and verification code.\par
7. Public verifiers shall verify certificates using token ID and verification code.\par
8. The public registry shall show aggregate statistics without exposing sensitive learner data.\par
9. Authorized issuers shall be able to revoke certificates.\par
\par
\b 3.2.2 Non-Functional Requirements\b0\par
\par
\b Security\b0\par
\par
The system must protect credentials, private keys, learner records and certificate verification codes. JWT authentication is used for protected routes. Passwords are hashed. Environment variables are used for sensitive secrets such as private keys and OpenAI API keys. The blockchain contract enforces authorized issuer permissions before certificates are issued or revoked.\par
\par
\b Integrity\b0\par
\par
Assessment records, certificate metadata and blockchain events must preserve evidence of issuance. The assessment report hash is generated deterministically so that the same assessment report produces the same hash. Certificate records are linked to token ID, learner, issuer, assessment and verification code.\par
\par
\b Privacy\b0\par
\par
The public verification registry must not expose full learner personal information by default. Learner names and emails are hashed in the local database model, and public verification requires both token ID and verification code before limited certificate details are shown.\par
\par
\b Availability\b0\par
\par
The frontend, backend, AI service and blockchain layer should be deployable independently. If the LLM service fails, local assessment fallback allows the system to continue operating.\par
\par
\b Usability\b0\par
\par
The interface should support separate learner, issuerand verification workflows. Learners should not be required to understand blockchain details before taking assessments. Issuers should be able to create courses, codes, materials and assessments through the dashboard.\par
\par
\b Scalability\b0\par
\par
The system uses a modular architecture. The frontend is static, the backend is API-based, the AI service is separated, and blockchain operations are limited to certificate lifecycle events. This separation makes it easier to scale assessment processing and web requests independently.\par
\par
\b Maintainability\b0\par
\par
The codebase is separated into clear modules: frontend scripts, backend API routers, database models, services, AI assessment modules and smart contracts. This follows the design principle of separating responsibilities across system components.\par
\par
\b 3.3 System Design Methodology\b0\par
\par
The system design methodology adopted for this project is a hybrid of object-oriented analysis and prototyping. Dennis, Wixom and Roth emphasize that systems analysis and design should move through requirements, modelling, architecture design, interface design, data storage design and implementation. This project follows that logic, but applies it in an iterative manner because the system combines several technologies that must be tested together.\par
\par
The methodology involved the following stages:\par
\par
1. Problem identification: Certificate forgery and weak verification were identified as the central problems.\par
2. Requirement determination: Functional and non-functional requirements were derived from the needs of learners, issuers and verifiers.\par
3. Process modelling: The workflow was decomposed into registration, course creation, enrollment, assessment, grading, certificate issuance and verification.\par
4. Object modelling: System entities such as User, Learner, Issuer, Course, CourseCode, CourseEnrollment, AssessmentTemplate, Assessment and Certificate were identified.\par
5. Architecture design: The system was designed as a modular web, AI and blockchain architecture.\par
6. Interface design: Separate views were created for authentication, learner dashboard, issuerdashboard and public verification.\par
7. Data storage design: Persistent storage was designed for backend records, AI cache and blockchain registry references.\par
8. Implementation and testing: The modules were implemented and tested through local preview, API tests, smart contract tests and end-to-end workflow checks.\par
\par
\b 3.3.1 Justification for Methodology\b0\par
\par
The object-oriented and prototype-based methodology is suitable for this project because the system has multiple interacting objects and actors. Dennis, Wixom and Roth describe use cases as a way to understand how users interact with a system, and class modelling as a way to represent objects, attributes and relationships. These concepts are directly applicable to SkillCert because the system includes learners, issuers, courses, assessments, certificates and blockchain records.\par
\par
Prototyping is also appropriate because the research requires practical integration of AI, smart contracts, IPFS and web authentication. The correct behaviour of such a system cannot be fully validated through design diagrams alone. It must be implemented and tested as a working prototype. During development, several issues were discovered and corrected, including frontend route errors, API authentication issues, OpenAI timeout behaviour, ABI mismatch between backend and smart contract, and invalid LLM question distribution. This confirms the importance of iterative prototyping in a multi-component system.\par
\par
\b 3.3.2 Method of Data Collection\b0\par
\par
The data used for system analysis was collected through:\par
\par
1. Review of literature on blockchain-based certificate verification, NFTs, smart contracts and AI assessment.\par
2. Review of the system analysis and design concepts in Dennis, Wixom and Roth.\par
3. Observation of common certificate issuance and verification workflows.\par
4. Analysis of the project requirements derived from the research aim and objectives.\par
5. Testing of the implemented prototype through local issuerand learner workflows.\par
\par
\b 3.4 System Modelling Using UML\b0\par
\par
UML modelling is used to describe the system in a way that is understandable to developers, stakeholders and evaluators. Dennis, Wixom and Roth explain that analysis models help analysts represent requirements and processes before or during construction. This study applies use case, activity, class and sequence modelling concepts to describe the system.\par
\par
\b 3.4.1 Use Case Model\b0\par
\par
The major actors are:\par
\par
1. Learner: Registers, logs in, views courses, enrolls with code, takes assessment and views results.\par
2. Issuer: Registers, logs in, creates courses, generates codes, uploads materials, creates assessment templates and monitors learners.\par
3. Public verifier: Views registry statistics and verifies a certificate using token ID and verification code.\par
4. AI service: Ingests material, generates questions, receives answers and grades assessments.\par
5. Blockchain registry: Issues, verifies and revokes NFT certificates.\par
6. IPFS service: Stores certificate metadata and PDF/evidence files.\par
7. System administrator: Manages authorized issuers and deployment configuration.\par
\par
