\b 3.5 System Design\b0\par
\par
\b 3.5.1 Overall Architecture\b0\par
\par
The system uses a modular architecture with four main layers:\par
\par
1. Presentation layer: Browser-based frontend built with HTML, CSS and JavaScript.\par
2. Application layer: FastAPI backend handling authentication, courses, assessments, certificates and API orchestration.\par
3. AI service layer: Separate FastAPI microservice handling material ingestion, question generation, grading and report hashing.\par
4. Blockchain and storage layer: Solidity smart contracts on Arbitrum Sepolia and optional IPFS pinning for metadata/PDF evidence.\par
\par
This architecture follows the architecture design idea described by Dennis, Wixom and Roth, where the analyst must specify how software, hardware, network and security components are arranged to satisfy requirements. The separation of frontend, backend, AI service and blockchain makes the system easier to maintain and test.\par
\par
Figure 3.5: System Architecture of SkillCert\par
Insert an architecture diagram showing Frontend, Backend API, Database, AI Service, OpenAI API, IPFS, Arbitrum Sepolia and Smart Contracts.\par
\par
\b 3.5.2 Input Design\b0\par
\par
Input design focuses on how data enters the system. In the user interface design discussion of Dennis, Wixom and Roth, input validation is important because poor input design can cause wrong data to enter the system. The SkillCert input design includes:\par
\par
1. Registration inputs: email, password, wallet address, role and issuer/learner profile fields.\par
2. Course inputs: title and description.\par
3. Course code inputs: quota and expiry settings.\par
4. Material inputs: title, programme, topics and material content or uploaded file.\par
5. Assessment inputs: assessment template selection and learner answers.\par
6. Verification inputs: token ID and verification code.\par
7. Blockchain configuration inputs: RPC URL, contract addresses and private key stored in .env.\par
\par
Important validation rules include unique email, unique wallet address, valid enrollment code, active assessment template, course enrollment requirement and hidden backend-controlled difficulty.\par
\par
\b 3.5.3 Output Design\b0\par
\par
The system outputs are:\par
\par
1. Course lists for learners.\par
2. Course, code and learner statistics for issuers.\par
3. Assessment questions.\par
4. Assessment score, outcome and feedback.\par
5. Score breakdown showing final, semantic and local scores.\par
6. Certificate NFT token ID and transaction hash.\par
7. Certificate PDF.\par
8. Public registry statistics.\par
9. Verification response showing valid or invalid certificate status.\par
\par
The output design follows the principle that outputs should support decision-making. Learners need to know their assessment result; issuers need to monitor courses and learners; verifiers need simple proof of certificate validity.\par
\par
\b 3.5.4 Database Design\b0\par
\par
The backend database is implemented using SQLAlchemy ORM. During local preview, SQLite is used, but the design can be migrated to PostgreSQL for production. The major tables include users, learners, issuers, courses, course codes, course enrollments, materials, assessment templates, assessments, certificates, audit logs and issuerkeys.\par
\par
The database design supports referential links among users, courses, assessments and certificates. For example, a certificate links to learner, issuerand assessment. This relationship ensures that a certificate is not an isolated record but part of a traceable evidence chain.\par
\par
\b 3.5.5 Smart Contract Design\b0\par
\par
The smart contract design contains two contracts:\par
\par
1. CertificationNFT.sol: An ERC721 certificate token contract. It mints SkillCert NFTs and prevents post-mint transfer, making the certificate soulbound.\par
2. CertificationRegistry.sol: A registry contract that authorizes issuers, issues certificates, stores certificate CIDs, verifies certificates and supports revocation.\par
\par
The registry uses issuer authorization so that only approved addresses can issue or revoke certificates. The verification function returns certificate validity, metadata CID, artefact CID, issuerDID and timestamp. This design provides public verifiability while keeping bulky and sensitive data off-chain.\par
\par
\b 3.5.6 AI Assessment Design\b0\par
\par
The AI assessment service is designed as a separate microservice. It performs:\par
\par
1. Material ingestion.\par
2. Key concept extraction using TF-IDF.\par
3. LLM-based question generation using OpenAI where available.\par
4. Local fallback question generation.\par
5. Answer submission storage.\par
6. Hybrid grading.\par
7. Assessment report hashing.\par
\par
The assessment contains exactly 30 questions: eight definition, eight conceptual, seven application and seven analysis. The expected answers and rubrics are stored internally and not exposed to learners.\par
\par
\b 3.5.7 Security Design\b0\par
\par
Security is addressed through:\par
\par
1. Password hashing.\par
2. JWT authentication and refresh tokens.\par
3. Role-based dashboard access.\par
4. Hidden assessment difficulty.\par
5. Server-side storage of expected answers and rubrics.\par
6. Verification code requirement for certificate lookup.\par
7. Smart contract issuer authorization.\par
8. .env protection for API keys and private keys.\par
9. Public registry aggregation without exposing sensitive learner details.\par
