\b\fs28 CHAPTER FOUR\b0\fs24\par
\par
\b\fs28 SYSTEM IMPLEMENTATION\b0\fs24\par
\par
\b 4.0 Introduction\b0\par
\par
This chapter describes the implementation of the Anti-Forgery Proof Certification Registry for Skill Acquisition Using Smart Contracts and Artificial Intelligence. While Chapter Three described the analysis and design, this chapter explains how the design was converted into a working prototype. It presents the development tools, programming languages, libraries, database implementation, AI implementation, smart contract implementation, frontend implementation, backend implementation, testing and results.\par
\par
The implementation follows the implementation phase described by Dennis, Wixom and Roth, where the system is constructed, tested and prepared for use. In this project, implementation required combining web development, API development, AI service integration, blockchain smart contracts and local preview testing.\par
\par
\b 4.1 Development Environment and Tools\b0\par
\par
The system was developed as a multi-service application. The main development environment and tools are:\par
\par
Component | Tool/Technology | Purpose\par
\par
Frontend | HTML, CSS, JavaScript | Browser interface for users.\par
Backend | Python FastAPI | API, database, authentication and orchestration.\par
AI Service | Python FastAPI | AI assessment generation and grading.\par
Database | SQLite locally, SQLAlchemy ORM | Persistent storage.\par
Smart Contracts | Solidity | NFT certificate and registry contracts.\par
Contract Framework | Foundry | Contract build and testing.\par
Blockchain | Arbitrum Sepolia | Testnet deployment and certificate issuance.\par
AI API | OpenAI gpt-4o-mini | Semantic question generation and grading.\par
Storage | IPFS/Pinata where configured | Metadata and certificate evidence.\par
Version Control | Git | Source code management.\par
\par
\b 4.1.1 Programming Languages and Libraries\b0\par
\par
The frontend uses HTML, CSS and JavaScript. The backend and AI service use Python. The contracts use Solidity version 0.8.20.\par
\par
Important backend libraries include:\par
\par
1. FastAPI for API routing.\par
2. SQLAlchemy for ORM database models.\par
3. Pydantic for request and response validation.\par
4. HTTPX for service-to-service requests.\par
5. Web3.py for blockchain communication.\par
6. JWT and password hashing utilities for authentication.\par
7. FPDF/qrcode-related utilities for certificate PDF generation where configured.\par
\par
Important AI service libraries include:\par
\par
1. scikit-learn for TF-IDF and local similarity scoring.\par
2. OpenAI Python SDK for LLM calls.\par
3. Pydantic for strict validation of LLM JSON.\par
4. SQLite persistence for AI service cache.\par
5. hashlib/json for deterministic assessment report hashing.\par
\par
Important contract libraries include OpenZeppelin ERC721 and AccessControl components.\par
\par
\b 4.1.2 Development Platform and Hardware Requirements\b0\par
\par
The system was developed and previewed locally on a Linux-based environment. The minimum hardware requirements for local testing are:\par
\par
1. Dual-core processor or better.\par
2. At least 4GB RAM.\par
3. Stable internet connection for OpenAI API, Arbitrum Sepolia RPC and IPFS pinning.\par
4. A modern browser such as Chrome.\par
5. Python virtual environments for backend and AI service.\par
6. Foundry installed for Solidity testing.\par
\par
\b 4.2 Dataset Description and Preprocessing\b0\par
\par
The prototype does not depend on a fixed public dataset. Instead, the issuerprovides learning material for each course. The AI service ingests this material and uses it as the basis for question generation and grading.\par
\par
The preprocessing steps include:\par
\par
1. Receiving material title, programme and content.\par
2. Storing material in the backend database.\par
3. Sending material to the AI service.\par
4. Extracting key concepts using TF-IDF.\par
5. Storing material and key concepts in the AI service persistence layer.\par
6. Using the material text and key concepts as input to the question generation process.\par
\par
The design is suitable for skill acquisition because different issuers can provide different materials for different courses. Each assessment template can therefore be generated from the material relevant to that course.\par
\par
\b 4.3 Model Architecture and Methodology\b0\par
\par
The AI model methodology is hybrid. It combines local rule-based/TF-IDF processing with LLM-based semantic generation and grading.\par
\par
\b 4.3.1 Question Generation Method\b0\par
\par
The system first extracts key concepts from the material. It then attempts LLM question generation. The LLM is instructed to return strict JSON. The response must contain exactly thirty questions with the following distribution:\par
\par
1. Eight definition questions.\par
2. Eight conceptual questions.\par
3. Seven application questions.\par
4. Seven analysis questions.\par
\par
Each internal question includes:\par
\par
1. question ID.\par
2. question text.\par
3. expected answer.\par
4. question type.\par
5. backend-controlled difficulty.\par
6. key concepts.\par
7. rubric.\par
8. points.\par
\par
However, expected answers and rubrics are removed from learner-facing responses. This prevents learners from seeing the answers before submission.\par
\par
\b 4.3.2 Local Fallback Method\b0\par
\par
If OpenAI is unavailable, times out or returns invalid JSON, the AI service falls back to local question generation. This makes the system more resilient because assessments can still be created even when the LLM service is unavailable.\par
\par
\b 4.3.3 Hybrid Grading Method\b0\par
\par
The grading process works as follows:\par
\par
1. Local grading runs for each answer.\par
2. LLM semantic grading runs in batch where OpenAI is available.\par
3. If LLM grading succeeds, the final score uses 85 percent LLM score and 15 percent local score.\par
4. If LLM grading fails, local grading is used fully.\par
5. The system returns final score, semantic score, local score, feedback and consistency notes.\par
\par
The local grader uses:\par
\par
1. TF-IDF cosine similarity.\par
2. Keyword overlap.\par
3. Completeness based on answer length.\par
4. Question-type rubrics for definition, conceptual, application and analysis questions.\par
\par
The LLM grader uses the question, expected answer, rubric and student answer. This improves fairness because learners may express correct answers using wording that differs from the course material.\par
\par
\b 4.3.4 Competency and Anomaly Detection\b0\par
\par
The codebase supports optional Random Forest competency classification and Isolation Forest anomaly detection. The model service checks for model artifacts:\par
\par
1. competency_model.joblib\par
2. anomaly_model.joblib\par
3. scaler.joblib\par
4. feature_names.json\par
\par
If the artifacts are missing or incompatible, the system logs a warning and continues without crashing. During the current prototype preview, these artifacts are not present, so competency and anomaly checks are disabled. The design still preserves the extension point for future trained-model integration.\par
\par
\b 4.3.5 Assessment Report Hash\b0\par
\par
After grading, the AI service generates an assessment report hash. The hash is produced from deterministic JSON using SHA-256. The purpose is to create a stable evidence reference that can later be included in certificate metadata or pinned to IPFS.\par
\par
\b 4.4 System Implementation and Modules\b0\par
\par
\b 4.4.1 Frontend Module\b0\par
\par
The frontend is located in frontend/public. It provides pages and scripts for:\par
\par
1. Authentication.\par
2. Learner dashboard.\par
3. issuerdashboard.\par
4. Public verification dashboard.\par
\par
The learner dashboard allows learners to view available courses, enroll with a code, start assessments, answer questions and view results. The issuerdashboard allows issuers to create courses, generate codes, add materials, create assessment templates and monitor learners. The verification dashboard allows public certificate checks.\par
\par
\b 4.4.2 Backend Module\b0\par
\par
The backend is located in backend/app. It is implemented with FastAPI and SQLAlchemy. The major API modules include:\par
\par
1. auth.py for registration, login, refresh token, logout and password recovery endpoints.\par
2. courses.py for course listing, enrollment and learner course retrieval.\par
3. issuers.py for course creation, code generation, material upload, template creation and learner result export.\par
4. assessments.py for material ingestion, assessment creation, answer submission, grading and result retrieval.\par
5. certificates.py for registry statistics, verification, revocation and learner certificate lookup.\par
\par
The backend also contains services for blockchain calls, IPFS pinning, certificate PDF generation and issuerkey management.\par
\par
\b 4.4.3 AI Service Module\b0\par
\par
The AI service is located in ai_service/app. Its major files include:\par
\par
1. main.py: FastAPI endpoints.\par
2. assessment.py: material store, question generation, answer submission and grading engine.\par
3. llm_service.py: OpenAI integration and strict JSON validation.\par
4. model_service.py: optional competency and anomaly model loading.\par
5. persistence.py: SQLite persistence for AI cache.\par
6. hash_utils.py: deterministic assessment report hashing.\par
\par
The AI service runs independently on port 8001.\par
\par
\b 4.4.4 Smart Contract Module\b0\par
\par
The contracts are located in contracts/src.\par
\par
CertificationNFT.sol implements the SkillCert NFT. It uses ERC721 URI storage and restricts minting to the registry contract. It also prevents transfer after minting, making the certificate non-transferable.\par
\par
CertificationRegistry.sol manages issuer authorization, certificate issuance, verification and revocation. It stores certificate metadata CID, assessment artefact CID, issuerDID and issuance timestamp.\par
\par
\b 4.4.5 Blockchain Service Module\b0\par
\par
The backend blockchain service uses Web3.py to connect to Arbitrum Sepolia. It loads the registry contract ABI and signs certificate issuance transactions. It supports issuer authorization checks, certificate issuance, verification and revocation.\par
\par
\b 4.4.6 Certificate PDF and Verification Module\b0\par
\par
When a learner passes an assessment, the backend generates certificate metadata and a PDF certificate. The PDF includes learner wallet, issuer, course, assessment, score, NFT token ID and verification code. The verification dashboard requires token ID and verification code before showing certificate validity.\par
\par
\b 4.5 Testing and Evaluation\b0\par
\par
Testing was carried out at different levels.\par
\par
\b 4.5.1 Smart Contract Testing\b0\par
\par
Foundry tests were written for the smart contracts. The tests cover:\par
\par
1. Admin authorizing issuers.\par
2. Authorized issuer issuing a certificate.\par
3. Unauthorized issuer being prevented from issuing a certificate.\par
4. Authorized issuer revoking a certificate.\par
5. Soulbound NFT transfer prevention.\par
\par
The tests passed after synchronizing the smart contract ABI and backend blockchain service.\par
\par
\b 4.5.2 API and Integration Testing\b0\par
\par
The backend and AI services were tested through local API calls. Confirmed checks include:\par
\par
1. AI health endpoint showing OpenAI key configured.\par
2. OpenAI model reachability for gpt-4o-mini.\par
3. Real LLM question generation producing exactly thirty questions.\par
4. Backend health connecting to Arbitrum Sepolia.\par
5. Course creation and enrollment through API.\par
6. Assessment creation after enrollment.\par
\par
\b 4.5.3 Frontend Testing\b0\par
\par
The frontend was tested locally through:\par
\par
1. issuerregistration and login.\par
2. Course creation.\par
3. Code generation.\par
4. Material ingestion.\par
5. Assessment template creation.\par
6. Learner registration and enrollment.\par
7. Assessment start and question display.\par
8. Result display.\par
9. Verification dashboard access.\par
\par
\b 4.5.4 Error Handling and Debugging\b0\par
\par
Several development issues were identified and fixed:\par
\par
1. Frontend switchTab reference error.\par
2. Wrong dashboard redirection after login.\par
3. Unauthorized course creation caused by stale or missing tokens.\par
4. Course enrollment fetch error caused by service/API availability.\par
5. Incorrect verification statistics caused by counting course codes incorrectly.\par
6. Backend AI request timeout during 30-question LLM generation.\par
7. OpenAI API key not visible to AI service.\par
8. LLM returning invalid question distribution.\par
9. Backend DEBUG=release parsing crash.\par
10. Smart contract ABI mismatch.\par
11. Foundry private key missing 0x prefix.\par
12. Real secret accidentally appearing in an example env file.\par
\par
These issues were important because they tested the reliability of the architecture and forced the implementation to become more robust.\par
